@startuml
' Source of truth for 03-read-flow.png -- see ../README.md to regenerate.
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam shadowing false
autonumber
title Read (decrypt) flow

participant "SQL client" as C
participant "FE\n(StarRocks)" as FE
participant "Iceberg lib\n(in the FE process)" as ICE
participant "KMS" as KMS
participant "BE" as BE
participant "Object store" as OBJ

C -> FE : SELECT from an encrypted iceberg table
note over FE
  Gate first, in buildScanRange, before any per-file test: a table
  that declares encryption.key-id while the catalog supplied a
  PlaintextEncryptionManager is refused. Such a table's files carry
  NO key metadata, so a per-file check would skip them and serve
  them silently -- the exact fail-open the gate exists for.
end note

== Planning: reach the data file's key_metadata ==
FE -> ICE : plan files for the snapshot
ICE -> OBJ : read metadata.json (NOT encrypted)
note over ICE
  snapshot.key-id gives the KEK_ID; encryption-keys holds the
  wrapped KEK and the encrypted manifest-list key metadata
end note
ICE -> KMS : unwrap the KEK with the table master key
KMS --> ICE : plaintext KEK
note over ICE
  decrypt the manifest-list key metadata with the KEK
  (AAD = KEK creation timestamp) -> manifest-list key
end note
ICE -> OBJ : read manifest list as AES-GCM stream (AGS1)
ICE -> OBJ : read manifest as AES-GCM stream (AGS1)
ICE --> FE : data files + their key_metadata
note over KMS : one KMS call per KEK, not per file

== Recover the per-file key ==
note over FE
  Parse StandardKeyMetadata directly for the DEK and AAD prefix.
  NOT via EncryptionManager.decrypt(): that returns a
  StandardDecryptedInputFile, which implements
  NativeEncryptionInputFile and NOT NativelyEncryptedFile, and
  exposes no nativeCryptoParameters() -- so there is no file key
  to take from it. No KMS round-trip and no DEK cache here.
  Failure to recover a key fails the query; never plaintext.
end note

== Data file ==
FE -> BE : scan range with PLAINTEXT file_dek + aad_prefix
BE -> OBJ : read encrypted footer
note over BE
  footer key = DEK, file_aad = aad_prefix + aad_file_unique.
  Caches an immutable EncryptionContext on the FileMetaData --
  algorithm, AAD inputs, key_metadata. NOT the DEK: that object
  goes into the process-wide footer cache, so caching the key
  would both retain it there and let a cache hit skip the
  "no key from the planner" refusal. The key comes from the
  scan range on every scan instead.
end note
BE -> OBJ : read encrypted page headers and page data
note over BE
  per-reader decryptor built from that context -- the parquet
  Decryptor mutates AAD per page and must not be shared --
  with per-page AAD recomputed via CreateModuleAad
end note
note over BE
  Page index and bloom filters are separate PME modules, each
  decrypted under its own AAD (module type, row group, column,
  kNonPageOrdinal) with the footer key under GCM. So pruning
  works on encrypted files too. A wrong module type or ordinal
  FAILS authentication rather than returning a structure that
  would then be used to index another.
end note
note over BE
  Page AAD uses the file's REAL page index, so the page-index
  skip path stays decryptable: a counter of headers read would
  desync the moment a page is skipped.
end note

== Delete files (each has its OWN key) ==
note over FE
  A delete file in an encrypted table is itself encrypted and
  carries its own per-file DEK. It CANNOT reuse the data file's
  key, so its key material travels separately.
end note
FE -> BE : position deletes: TIcebergDeleteFile.parquet_encryption_info
FE -> BE : equality deletes: their own scan range, encrypted like a data file
BE -> OBJ : read + decrypt the delete files
note over BE
  Only Parquet position deletes can be decrypted; an encrypted
  ORC position-delete file is rejected rather than misread.
end note

BE --> C : decrypted rows
@enduml
